  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(235.036pt, 472.08pt, "Cs decays, this histogram exhibits a pronounced Compton edge at ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 491.04pt, "477 keV, which represents the maximum energy deposition of Compton electrons due to scattered 662 ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 510pt, "keV gamma rays.  ")
  #line-at(81.6pt, 535.2pt, "The Compton edge in a measured pulse-height spectrum is not an ideal step function but the result of ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 554.16pt, "(i) the underlying Compton electron energy distribution, (ii) multiple scattering and escape processes, ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 573.12pt, "and (iii) convolution with the detector response function. The resulting structure exhibits a characteristic ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 592.08pt, "asymmetric curvature with an inflection, and a single reversal point in its first derivative. An established ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 611.04pt, "method, proposed by Dietze and Klein [19], is fitting the experimental Compton edge against Monte-")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 630pt, "Carlo simulations convolved with a resolution function. This is physically well motivated, but it requires ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 648.96pt, "an accurate modeling of detector geometry and material composition, source location and geometry, ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 667.92pt, "assumptions about the energy-dependent resolution function, and reliable normalization and background ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 686.88pt, "treatment. Mismatches in these inputs can introduce systematic biases comparable to, or larger than, the ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 705.84pt, "statistical uncertainty of the edge determination. However, in the present study all materials are measured ")
]

