#original-page(595.2pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 7.92pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(228.055pt, 116.4pt, "Paper submitted to ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 7.92pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(290.047pt, 116.4pt, "Radiation Measurement ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 7.92pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(69.6pt, 116.4pt, "10 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(69.6pt, 143.04pt, "uncertainties. Only the overall FOM trend (Figure 5) indicates a minor degradation above 500 keVee for ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 162pt, "the EJ-276D and M700 materials, while M600 seems unaffected. ")
  #line-at(81.6pt, 186.96pt, "Figure 6 summarizes the discrimination performance of the three PSD scintillators before and after ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 205.92pt, "ageing in terms of their FOM plotted versus energy deposition. M700 provides the best separation, ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 224.88pt, "followed by EJ-276D and M600. This picture does not change with the ageing as performed in this work.  ")
  #line-at(81.6pt, 249.84pt, "Characteristic pulse shapes of neutron- and gamma-induced signals in EJ-276D and M700 are given ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 268.8pt, "in Figure 7. The graphs represent sums of all pulses with energy depositions between 452 and 502 keVee ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 287.76pt, "which are identified as neutron or gamma-ray responses, respectively, with the pulse maximum ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 306.72pt, "normalized to 1. For comparison, a normalized sum of single-electron pulses (SEP) is also shown, ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 325.68pt, "together with a SEP model representing a delta function (the initial photoelectron emission), convolved ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 344.88pt, "with a Gaussian (")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(149.625pt, 344.88pt, "𝜎= 7")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(176.748pt, 344.88pt, " ns) representing the PMT response to a single photoelectron, the ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(478.007pt, 344.88pt, "𝜏= 9.5")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(512.054pt, 344.88pt, " ns ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 364.08pt, "exponential decay of the fast RC filter at the oscilloscope input (300 MHz bandwidth limitation), and the ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(69.6pt, 383.28pt, "𝜏= 3 𝜇")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(104.049pt, 383.28pt, "s differentiated exponential due to the AC-coupled anode readout. This model describes the SEP ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 402.24pt, "response very well. On the one hand it is obvious that the leading part of all scintillator signals is strongly ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 421.2pt, "affected, in fact rather determined, by this SEP response. On the other hand, the pulse tail is indeed very ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 440.16pt, "different for the gamma- and neutron induced signals, but also for the distinct scintillator materials. The ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 459.12pt, "M700 signals are distinguished by a shorter decay, which generally allows a shorter full-charge ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 478.32pt, "integration time. The latter is of importance for applications where high detector loads must be handled, ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 497.28pt, "as shorter integration gates reduce the dead time per event and increase the maximum throughput of the ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 516.24pt, "pulse-processing electronics. ")
  #line-at(81.6pt, 541.2pt, "The light yields relative to the EJ-200 sample, and the FOM values, both determined for the 477 keV ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 560.16pt, "Compton edge before and after the ageing process, are compiled in ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.08pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(367.1pt, 560.16pt, "Table 2")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(397.363pt, 560.16pt, ".  ")
  #line-at(81.6pt, 585.12pt, "The light yields of EJ-200, EJ-276D, and M700 are very similar, and they are only marginally affected ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 604.08pt, "by the “steaming” and “freezing” processes. The light yield of M600 is generally lower. While M600 ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 623.04pt, "also shows a smaller FOM, reflecting a lower PSD performance, the M700 can compete with EJ-276D ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 642pt, "in all respects. ")
  #line-at(81.6pt, 666.96pt, "The relatively short ageing procedure applied here is obviously not sufficient to disclose differences ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 685.92pt, "in the long-term stability of various materials. Likewise, this study has not touched properties as stress ")
  #line-at(69.6pt, 704.88pt, "behavior, breaking resistance, elasticity, plasticity – properties that determine the care and expense ")
]

